WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.200
Why can't Sharkboy breathe underwater? And&nbsp;
why would supposedly valiant heroes steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.240
cookies? Here are a few things&nbsp;
about "The Adventures of Sharkboy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.160
and Lavagirl" that people only seem&nbsp;
to think about once they've grown up.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.480
In the mid-2000s, Robert Rodriguez was&nbsp;
arguably at the height of his powers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.120
The Texas-born filmmaker had gone from&nbsp;
producing "El Mariachi" for a mere seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.960
thousand dollars in 1992 to developing beloved&nbsp;
hits like "Desperado," "From Dusk Till Dawn,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.000
and of course, the "Spy Kids" films. However,&nbsp;
"The Adventures of Sharkboy and Lavagirl" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.800
undeniably a low point in the director's career.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.840
Even the most inane, toilet-humor-based kids&nbsp;
movies will often throw in some jokes for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.400
parents. "Sharkboy and Lavagirl," however,&nbsp;
is aimed squarely at the kids — and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.960
because it was partially written by one. The&nbsp;
opening titles of "Sharkboy and Lavagirl"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.840
introduce it as "a Rodriguez family movie,"&nbsp;
based on the dreams of then-7-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.000
Racer Max Rodriguez, who also played&nbsp;
young Sharkboy in the opening scene.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.320
The adults in the movie, although played&nbsp;
by household-name actors like George Lopez,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.760
David Arquette, and Kristin Davis, still deliver&nbsp;
stiff, awkward dialogue that sounds like a child's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.320
idea of the way an adult might speak. For&nbsp;
an adult hoping for action like "Desperado,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.120
a compelling narrative like "Sin City," or&nbsp;
special effects like "Alita: Battle Angel,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.360
"Sharkboy and Lavagirl" is certainly one&nbsp;
of the less compelling Rodriguez films.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:28.160
The word "dream" — or some variation of it&nbsp;
— is uttered a total of 188 times throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.680
"Sharkboy and Lavagirl's" 93-minute run time.&nbsp;
That means the word is being said, on average,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.080
once every thirty seconds — but in some&nbsp;
scenes, it's much more often than that.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.200
Even by the fiftieth time, it still isn't very&nbsp;
clear what the movie has to say about dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.360
other than that they're a good thing to have.&nbsp;
Young Max is mocked by his classmates for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.480
reading stories about Sharkboy and Lavagirl from&nbsp;
his dream journal — but then, his dreams turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.440
out to be real, so Linus and the other kids&nbsp;
learn that dreams are good because… they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.880
become real? But Max's dream almost destroys the&nbsp;
school, and that doesn't sound very good at all.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.120
There's also the matter of Max's parents.&nbsp;
His father is an unemployed writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.840
and his inability to get a reliable job has&nbsp;
pushed his parents to the brink of divorce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.920
At the film's climax, rather than&nbsp;
finding some sort of mature compromise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.320
the pair have a near-death discussion where they&nbsp;
remind each other that they're best friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.520
reconciling without actually resolving the issue&nbsp;
at hand. Rather than have Max's father figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.800
out a way to follow his dreams while still&nbsp;
supporting his family, the movie just… ends.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.320
"Sharkboy and Lavagirl" makes such a&nbsp;
repetitive point about the importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.360
of dreaming that it seems like it should actually&nbsp;
have something to say by the end of it. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.360
all this talk of dreams eventually&nbsp;
starts to sound more like a nightmare.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.120
"The Adventures of Sharkboy and Lavagirl" is, on&nbsp;
the one hand, refreshingly colorful and vivid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.840
During a time when even kid-targeted action&nbsp;
movies were slowly inching towards gloomy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.440
dystopian franchises like "The Maze&nbsp;
Runner" and "The Hunger Games," the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.200
colorful world of Drool stood out as a&nbsp;
unique vision. On the other hand, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.640
an adult watches "Sharkboy and Lavagirl," they&nbsp;
see a film drunk on its own greenscreen power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.960
one that makes you wonder how it could&nbsp;
have possibly cost $50 million to produce.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.920
At the time of the film's release in&nbsp;
2005, CGI was being used more and more in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.560
all sorts of movies, but the technology was&nbsp;
at the end of an awkward transitional phase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.520
"Sharkboy and Lavagirl" was shot almost entirely&nbsp;
in front of a green screen — and it shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.240
with its more fantastical settings resembling&nbsp;
a Windows screensaver more than a major motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:36.800
picture. That's not to say that CGI-heavy movies&nbsp;
can't be good — 1982's "TRON" and 2008's "Speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.320
Racer" are both underrated gems — but as an&nbsp;
adult watching "Sharkboy and Lavagirl" in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.680
it's hard to not be distracted by visual effects&nbsp;
less sophisticated than an Instagram filter.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.600
Additionally, the 3D technology&nbsp;
used for "Sharkboy and Lavagirl"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.240
made for a good selling point, but did&nbsp;
little to actually make the film look good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.000
"Sharkboy and Lavagirl" utilized anaglyph 3D — the&nbsp;
kind that uses those red and blue glasses — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.880
just made the movie look even worse on the big&nbsp;
screen. As Roger Ebert put it in his review,

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.600
"Fully three quarters of the movie is in&nbsp;
'3'-D, which looks more like 1-D to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:15.760
removing the brightness and life of the&nbsp;
movie's colors and replacing them with a drab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.000
listless palette, which is about as exciting&nbsp;
as looking at a 3-D bowl of oatmeal."

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.520
In 2005, George Lopez was at the top of his&nbsp;
game. He was gearing up for the fifth season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.920
of his highly-rated sitcom "George Lopez,"&nbsp;
he'd made cameos in shows like "Curb Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.080
Enthusiasm," and he'd established himself&nbsp;
as a well-respected stand-up comedian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.400
In "Sharkboy and Lavagirl," Lopez fully commits to&nbsp;
his dual role as Max's teacher Mr. Electricidad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.400
as well as one of the chief bad&nbsp;
guys on Planet Drool, Mr. Electric.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.720
Both roles are over the top, of course,&nbsp;
and Lopez mostly pulls them off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.560
Highlights include his demand for Max&nbsp;
and his bully to be friends as a teacher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.920
or his constant spouting of electricity-based&nbsp;
puns as a floating orb with claws for hands.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:58.880
"Hey, watts up? You know, watts!"

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.840
But why did George Lopez also have to&nbsp;
provide a voiceover for Max's childhood robot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.000
Tobor? Following the success of films&nbsp;
like "Aladdin" and "Shrek," Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
had developed something of an obsession&nbsp;
with casting movie stars in voice roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.840
largely ignoring an entire industry of&nbsp;
talented voice professionals who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.840
been providing their talents to cartoons&nbsp;
and animated movies for decades prior.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.120
So, rather than finding the perfect voice actor&nbsp;
to design an endearing identity for Tobor around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:30.080
the audience gets George Lopez again — and&nbsp;
yet again for a fourth time as a guardian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.400
of the Ice Princess. Robert Rodriguez&nbsp;
himself even voices a talking shark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.560
further trading creativity for&nbsp;
mediocre, uninspired stunt casting.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.320
Out of everyone in "Sharkboy&nbsp;
and Lavagirl," it's clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.480
future "Twilight" heartthrob Taylor&nbsp;
Lautner is the actor who possesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:51.120
"it," that indefinable quality that&nbsp;
makes a movie star, well, a movie star.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.880
When production on the film began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.280
Lautner was just 12 years old. He already&nbsp;
had an extensive martial arts background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.240
earning a black belt by age 8 and appearing&nbsp;
in a national karate tournament in 2003.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.160
Lautner was the first and only actor&nbsp;
to audition for the role of Sharkboy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.760
Rodriguez was truly convinced he&nbsp;
was perfect from the very beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:15.280
Sure enough, Lautner's Sharkboy is a glowering&nbsp;
menace: he never stops moving and rages easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.920
snarling and yelling without holding back. He&nbsp;
plays an important role in the most standout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.200
action scene of the film, using his martial arts&nbsp;
skills in an elaborate fight with Mr. Electric's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:30.960
dog-like cord monsters. Rodriguez even asked&nbsp;
Lautner to choreograph his own fight scenes.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.120
Lautner also takes center stage for one of&nbsp;
the most bizarre and memorable moments of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.840
"Sharkboy and Lavagirl:" a ridiculous,&nbsp;
inexplicably aggressive lullaby that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.800
sings to get Max to fall asleep and dream&nbsp;
planet Drool back to its rightful shape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.520
He busts impressive dance moves while&nbsp;
sing-yelling all sorts of absurd lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.000
It's a shame that Taylor Lautner couldn't&nbsp;
return for "We Can Be Heroes," the 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.200
quasi-sequel to "Sharkboy and Lavagirl," as he&nbsp;
was undoubtedly the true star of the original.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.520
You'd think that the whole point&nbsp;
of having elements of Max's dream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.520
world echo elements of Max's real&nbsp;
world would be to illuminate his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.840
relationships with the people and things&nbsp;
that surround him. Lavagirl, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.120
asks Max why he made her Sharkboy's partner&nbsp;
when they're, quote, "not compatible" — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.160
same words Max's mother used to describe why&nbsp;
she and Max's father are not getting along.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.760
Such moments could, if properly&nbsp;
and somewhat clearly presented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.080
give a film like this greater depth.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, Sharkboy and Lavagirl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.640
breeze past the questions as quickly as they&nbsp;
bring it up. The additional presence of Max's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:40.480
parents as cookie-hungry giants on planet Drool&nbsp;
just serves to make things even more confusing.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.000
Then there's Mr. Electricidad, who allows his&nbsp;
daughter Marissa to face off against a giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.920
robot with his own face on it. While&nbsp;
the idea of a girl facing off against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.720
an imaginary construct of her own father&nbsp;
seems like it could be rich with symbolism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.680
such moments don't seem to&nbsp;
connect to any greater theme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.960
other than revolving around a loose, Freudian&nbsp;
concept of dreams being inherently meaningful.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.400
"The Adventures of Sharkboy and Lavagirl"&nbsp;
has a few off-putting attempts at the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.520
wink-wink adult humor other children's movies&nbsp;
sometimes tackle with wit and subtlety. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:18.560
however, they come across as awkward at&nbsp;
best, and just plain confusing at worst.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.520
At one point, Sharkboy says he's heard that&nbsp;
the Ice Princess is the "most beautiful girl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.280
on the planet," causing Lavagirl to&nbsp;
get jealous and zap him in the butt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:31.760
with her lava powers. Other moments&nbsp;
include Mr. Electricidad's insistence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.800
that Max stays away from his daughter, and&nbsp;
Linus' reaction to Lavagirl burning his desk.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:37.280
"She's hot!"
[shredding paper]

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.600
During moments like these, it feels like you&nbsp;
can see remnants of a screenplay with jokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.160
intended for adults, but they're ultimately more&nbsp;
half-baked than the cookies in Max's kitchen.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.400
In "We Can Be Heroes," it is revealed that&nbsp;
the pair eventually got together and had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:56.240
child of their own. At some point, it seems that&nbsp;
what was once implied has now become explicit.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.920
It might seem like a minor detail, but early on in&nbsp;
"Sharkboy and Lavagirl," Max wakes up to find that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.120
the cookies his mom baked and left in the kitchen&nbsp;
have been strangely consumed. One of the cookies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.560
has jagged, shark-like teeth marks in it, while&nbsp;
the other is burnt and remains hot to the touch.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:16.480
His parents, of course, don't believe&nbsp;
his repeated insistence that it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.080
him who ate the baked treats — and&nbsp;
clearly, judging from the evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.280
it was Sharkboy and Lavagirl. It's not until&nbsp;
the next day that the duo reveal themselves as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:30.560
real heroes, recruiting Max to help them&nbsp;
save planet Drool while he's asleep in class.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.280
As cute as the scene might be, it also begs a&nbsp;
question: Why bother sneaking into his house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.520
the night before they were going to contact&nbsp;
him anyway? Are the heroes like Santa Claus?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:42.640
Did they think it would be funny&nbsp;
to get Max into trouble? Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.440
has Max seemingly discovered that Sharkboy&nbsp;
and Lavagirl are real, but in this scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.880
he also learns that they have no&nbsp;
issues with chocolate chip thievery.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:57.120
After he wraps up his battle with Minus on&nbsp;
Drool, Lavagirl tells Max to blink three times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.480
which will take him home — much like&nbsp;
Dorothy clicking her heels together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.400
in "The Wizard of Oz." When he does&nbsp;
so, he wakes up back in his classroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:08.240
implying that a large portion of what&nbsp;
we've just seen was a dream of some sort.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:13.280
But during that "dream," Max fell asleep and&nbsp;
had another dream inside of that dream. Is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:18.560
even possible? It seems like "The Adventures of&nbsp;
Sharkboy and Lavagirl" is a lot like "Inception,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:23.920
but without Leonardo DiCaprio patiently explaining&nbsp;
how things work. There are dreams within dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:29.120
and then those dreams intrude on what you thought&nbsp;
was reality all along. Like dreams themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:33.360
the whole thing is as impossible to&nbsp;
follow as it is to explain to anyone else.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:39.200
This is yet another nitpick, but it's the sort of&nbsp;
thing that can really nag an adult. Up until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.880
very last battle on Drool, it is made abundantly&nbsp;
clear that Sharkboy possesses the abilities of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:49.360
well, a shark. After being lost at&nbsp;
sea, Sharkboy was raised by sharks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:53.760
growing sharp teeth, fins, and gills&nbsp;
as he adapted to the shark lifestyle.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:57.040
However, in the big showdown&nbsp;
with Mr. Electricidad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:01.120
electric eels get the better of&nbsp;
Sharkboy, nearly causing him to drown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.240
You know, because sharks have to&nbsp;
watch out, or they could drown.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:04.415
"Can he survive down there?"

00:11:04.415 --> 00:11:04.880
"He can hold his breath, but not forever.&nbsp;
He'll drown if I don't go get him."

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.120
What about the gills that we saw all up and&nbsp;
down his sides, at the beginning of the film?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:14.400
It's an example of a screenplay giving a character&nbsp;
a newfound limitation, simply because it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:19.760
called for at that particular moment to build&nbsp;
dramatic tension. It's also frustratingly lazy.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:24.560
Nearly two decades later, "The Adventures of&nbsp;
Sharkboy and Lavagirl" continues to hold a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:28.000
special place in the hearts of those&nbsp;
who discovered it as kids. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:33.040
thanks to all sorts of bizarre moments and&nbsp;
choices, you'd probably be doing yourself a favor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:37.680
to keep the movie in your memories rather than&nbsp;
revisiting it on your streaming site of choice.

